{
  "pdf_page": 55,
  "book_page": 25,
  "verified_by": "claude-fable-5-1",
  "verified_on": "2026-09-04",
  "entries": [
    {
      "entry": 0,
      "root": "An 3, Anô",
      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "modern_form": "PIE *h₂en- / *h₂ana (older notation *ano-, *anē) 'on, upon, up along'",
      "note": "Pokorny's headword is '4. an, anu, and, no — Bedeutung: eine Präposition (\"an einer schrägen Fläche entlang o. dgl.\")', pp. 39-40; Watkins/AHD numbers exactly the same root 'an³. On. Extended form *ana … [Pok. 4. an 39.]', which is where Nourai's '3' and the second citation form 'Anô' (= Watkins's *anē) come from. Klein s.v. ana- posits 'I.-E. base *ano-, \"on, upon, above\"'. So the root, the language label (Indo-European) and the gloss are all correct; the only reservation on the whole entry concerns Old Persian anā (node 1), which Kent rejects as a preposition.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E1%BC%80%CE%BD%CE%AC",
        "https://www.etymonline.com/word/on"
      ],
      "ref_check": [
        {
          "ref": "POK:39",
          "status": "supports",
          "note": "POK_full.txt block ending 'Page(s): 39-40': 'Root / lemma: an 4, anu, and, no — Meaning: a preposition (\"along a slanted surface, etc.\") — Material: Avestan ana, Old pers. ana … \"along, on\" (m. Akk.), Avestan anu, Old pers. anuv \"after, according to; up there\" … References: WP. I 58 f., WH. I 43 f., 49, 677'. Exactly the root, gloss and first branch Nourai draws."
        }
      ],
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "lang": "Avestan / Old Persian",
          "words": "ana : on, upon; anu, anuv : to, after, along; anu-i : going along",
          "verdict": "plausible",
          "derivation": "Av. ana and (per Bartholomae) OP anā continue the PIE particle *h₂ana with acc./instr. 'over along, on'; Av. anu / OP anuv (with the Old Persian glide -v written for word-final -u, §, Kent's 'anuv (i.e. anu)') continue the -u extension *h₂enu, matching Skt. ánu 'after, along'. Both are inherited, not borrowed: intervocalic *-n- is stable in Iranian and the *a-vocalism is unchanged, so the Iranian forms are phonetically identical to the reconstructed particle. The third item 'anu-i \"going along\"' is not a lemma in Bartholomae or Kent; it looks like Nourai's citation form of the preverb anu- with the root ay-/i- 'to go' (cf. Skt. anv-i- 'to follow along', Av. anu + verbs of motion, e.g. anu.zafan- 'going along the muzzle'), i.e. a construction rather than a word — I could not confirm it as such. The one substantive problem is OP anā: Kent (Old Persian, 1950, Lexicon p. 168) parses the two attestations (DPe 8, XPa 14) as the instrumental singular of the demonstrative ²a- 'this' and states they are 'not to Av. Skt. ana-' and 'not prep.'; Bartholomae (AiW) and Pokorny do list 'p. anā' as the preposition. Nourai follows the older (Bartholomae/Pokorny) view.",
          "sources": [
            "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E1%BC%80%CE%BD%CE%AC"
          ],
          "ref_check": [
            {
              "ref": "POK:39",
              "status": "supports",
              "note": "POK_full.txt, 'Root / lemma: an 4, anu, and, no' (Page(s): 39-40): 'Avestan ana, Old pers. ana (Proto Aryan *ana or *ānā) \"about there\" (m. Akk. or Instr.), \"along, on\" (m. Akk.), Avestan anu, Old pers. anuv \"after, according to; up there\" (m. Akk.), \"lengthwise, along\" (m. Lok.), also proverb [= Präverb]'."
            },
            {
              "ref": "KLN:67",
              "status": "supports",
              "note": "KLN_1966.txt leaf 47 (= printed pp. 67-68, the leaf carries the numerals '67','68'), s.v. ana-: 'Gk. ἀνα-, ἀν-, \"up to, upward, up, toward; … back, backward; against\", fr. ἀνά … cogn. with Avestic ana, \"on, upon\", L. an- in anhelāre … fr. I.-E. base *ano-, \"on, upon, above\".' Klein gives the Avestan form but not the Old Persian."
            },
            {
              "ref": "KNT:164",
              "status": "partial",
              "note": "Kent's Old Persian lexicon does have the words, but on p. 168, not 164 (KNT.txt leaf 189; the neighbouring leaf 187 carries the printed numeral 166, so printed = leaf − 21): 'anuv (i.e. anu) prep. \"along, according to\": Av. anu with acc., loc., \"toward, along\", Skt. ánu with acc. \"after, along\"'. But the same page CONTRADICTS the first word: 'anā ism. to ²a- (q.v.); DPe 8 (not to Av. Skt. ana-); XPa 14 (not prep.)', and s.v. ²a- Kent adds that the Akkadian a-ga-' and Elamite hi 'this' renderings 'refute[] interpretation … as prep. w. inst. \"throughout\", Av. ana with acc. \"along, on\", Gk. ἀνά \"along\"'. Page 164 is the first page of the lexicon (headwords a-, akarta- etc.), so the number is a page or two off as well."
            },
            {
              "ref": "DZA:57",
              "status": "not_checked",
              "note": "Rashed Mohassel, Darāmadi bar Dastur-e Zabān-e Avestāʾi (Khajeh 1364/1985): refs_online.json records no digital copy (kind 'purchase', only a revised e-book on taaghche.com), and it is not in the local library."
            }
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {
              "src": "BRT",
              "where": "AiW col. ~124, s.v. ana (BRT.txt line 4208)",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'j. ana, p. anā Praep. \"über — hin\"; 1) mit Akk. a) \"über — hin\" … b) \"entlang, längs\"' — Bartholomae accepts both the Avestan and the Old Persian preposition. And s.v. anu (line 4484): 'j. anu, p. anuv, g. anū: 1) Praep. a) mit Akk. α) \"nach, gemäss\" … b) mit Lok. \"längs, entlang an —\" … 2) Praev.'. Exactly Nourai's 'anu, anuv'."
            },
            {
              "src": "KNT",
              "where": "Lexicon p. 168 (KNT.txt leaf 189)",
              "stance": "partial",
              "note": "confirms OP anuv as a preposition but explicitly denies that OP anā is this word (see ref_check above)."
            },
            {
              "src": "POK",
              "where": "IEW 39-40 s.v. 4. an, anu, and, no",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "as quoted above; also notes the -ī final that may lie behind Nourai's 'anu-i': 'Adv. \"on it\" (auslaut -ī appears to be comparable in Lesbian Thessalian [ὀνί] beside Attic ἀνά)'."
            },
            {
              "src": "IEC",
              "where": "Mann, s.v. ana (IEC.txt line 517)",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'ana (ono) \"on, upon\" WP I, 57 … Av. ana; … Gk. aná; Osc. ana-, an-; Go. OFris. ana.' Mann lists Avestan but no Old Persian."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "lang": "Germanic",
          "words": "ana, ano",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "Proto-Germanic *ana / *anē 'on' directly continues PIE *h₂ana with no change (Germanic keeps *a as a and *n as n; the root is unstressed and never underwent Verner or the vowel shifts that affect full lexical words). It is attested as Gothic ana (with dat. and acc.) 'on, in, against', Old Norse á, Old Saxon an, Old English on/an, Old High German ana/an > German an, Middle Dutch aan. Nourai's two citation forms 'ana, ano' are the short and lengthened stems (*ana, *anē), i.e. AHD's '*ana, anē'.",
          "sources": [
            "https://www.etymonline.com/word/on"
          ],
          "ref_check": [
            {
              "ref": "AHD",
              "status": "supports",
              "note": "AHD_watkins1985.txt, s.v. an³: 'an³. On. Extended form *ana. 1. Germanic *ana, anē in: a. Old English an, on, a, on: ON; b. Old Norse á, in, on: ALOFT; c. Old High German ana (> Middle High German ane), on: ANLAGE; d. Middle Dutch aan, on: ONSLAUGHT. 2. Greek ana, on, up, at the rate of: ANA², ANA-. [Pok. 4. an 39.]' — this single entry is the source of both of Nourai's branches (Germanic and Greek) and of the two forms 'ana, ano'."
            }
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {
              "src": "KLN",
              "where": "p. 67 s.v. ana-",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'cogn. with … Goth. ana, OE. on, an, OSlav. na, Lith. nu, OPruss. no, na, \"on, upon\"'."
            },
            {
              "src": "POK",
              "where": "IEW 39-40",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'Gothic ana (m. dat. mid Akk.) \"on, in, against, because of, about\", Old Norse á … Old Saxon an, Old English on, Old High German ana, an, Modern High German an (*ana or *anō, *anē) preposition m. dat. and Akk. and Instr.'"
            },
            {
              "src": "IEC",
              "where": "Mann, s.v. ana (IEC.txt line 517)",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'Go. OFris. ana'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "lang": "English",
          "words": "on, upon, unless",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "OE on (unstressed variant of an) 'in, on, into' < PGmc *ana; ME on > NE on with no further change. 'Upon' is the Middle English univerbation of up + on (13c., on the model of / reinforced by Old Norse upp á), so its second element is this same word. 'Unless' is not the negative prefix un- at all: it is a reshaping of Middle English 'on lesse (that)' 'in a less (case) that', with on- later assimilated to un- by folk-association with the privative prefix; so it too contains the preposition on. All three therefore belong under this root, which is the (slightly startling) point Nourai is making by listing them together.",
          "sources": [
            "https://www.etymonline.com/word/on"
          ],
          "ref_check": [
            {
              "ref": "None",
              "status": "not_checked",
              "note": "the chart gives this node no reference of its own; it inherits the AHD citation of its parent (Germanic) node, which does list 'Old English an, on, a, on: ON'."
            }
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {
              "src": "KLN",
              "where": "KLN_1966.txt line 40823, s.v. unless (leaf 890 ≈ p. 1756)",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'unless, conj. — Fr. earlier onlesse, onlesse that, i.e. \"on less that\". Cp. F. à moins que, \"unless\", lit. \"on less that\", and see on and less.' Klein derives unless from on, exactly as the chart implies."
            },
            {
              "src": "SKT",
              "where": "SKT.txt line 20415, s.v. Un- (1)",
              "stance": "partial",
              "note": "Skeat treats un- (1) as the negative prefix from A.S. un-, IE *n̥-, i.e. a different word; he does not connect un- to on. This is consistent with Klein: it is only in 'unless' specifically that the un- goes back to on-, not the productive prefix."
            },
            {
              "src": "AHD",
              "where": "Watkins 1985 s.v. an³",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'Old English an, on, a, on: ON'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "lang": "Greek",
          "words": "ana- : according to, upward, back, again; ana-luein : to loosen back, analyze; analogos : speak according to, give analogy",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "Gk. ἀνά / ἀνα- 'up, on, upon, throughout; back; again' continues *h₂ana unchanged (Attic-Ionic ἀνά, with the dialectal shortened forms Doric-Boeotian ἄν, Lesbian-Thessalian ὄν); it lost its independent prepositional use in Koine but survived as a very productive preverb. ἀναλύειν 'to unloose, release, set free' = ἀνα- 'back, up' + λύειν 'to loosen' (PIE *leu- 'to cut apart'), whence the verbal noun ἀνάλυσις 'a loosing, releasing', which Aristotle used technically for 'analysis'. ἀνάλογος is ἀνά + λόγος in the sense 'ratio, proportion' — so the correct gloss is 'proportionate, conformable, according to due ratio', not the chart's 'speak according to, give analogy'; that gloss reflects the other sense of λόγος ('speech'), and is the one loose spot in an otherwise correct node.",
          "sources": [
            "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E1%BC%80%CE%BD%CE%AC",
            "https://www.etymonline.com/word/analysis"
          ],
          "ref_check": [
            {
              "ref": "None",
              "status": "not_checked",
              "note": "the chart gives the Greek node no reference; its child (English) carries 'AHD', and AHD's an³ entry covers the Greek link ('2. Greek ana, on, up, at the rate of: ANA², ANA-')."
            }
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {
              "src": "KLN",
              "where": "p. 67 s.v. ana- and p. 70 s.v. analysis, analogy",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "s.v. analysis: 'Gk. ἀνάλυσις, \"a loosing, releasing\", fr. ἀναλύειν, \"to unloose, release, set free\", fr. ἀνά, \"up, on, throughout\" (see ana-), and λύειν, \"to unfasten, loosen, slacken\"'; s.v. analogy: 'F. analogie, fr. L. analogia, fr. Gk. ἀναλογία, \"proportion, analogy\", fr. ἀνάλογος' — note Klein's gloss 'proportion', confirming the correction above."
            },
            {
              "src": "POK",
              "where": "IEW 39-40",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'Ionic-Attic ἀνά, ἀνά \"on, upwards, along\", Doric Boeotian Arcadian Cypriot ἄν, Lesbian Thessalian Arcadian … ὄν … Adv. ἄνω \"upward, up\"'."
            },
            {
              "src": "IEC",
              "where": "Mann, s.v. ana",
              "stance": "supports",
              "note": "'Gk. aná'."
            }
